<section>
    <h3>What really goes out… and what is written</h3>
    <div class="before-after">
        <div>
            <p class="label-before">Real exchange</p>
            <pre><code class="language-http" data-trim>
POST /oauth/token
Authorization: Basic bXktYXBwOnMzY3IzdA==

grant_type=client_credentials
&amp;client_id=my-app&amp;client_secret=s3cr3t

200 OK
{"access_token":"eyJhbGciOi...",
 "token_type":"Bearer","expires_in":3600}
            </code></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="fragment">
            <p class="label-after">.har file</p>
            <pre><code class="language-http" data-trim>
POST /oauth/token
Authorization: [REDACTED]

grant_type=client_credentials
&amp;client_id=my-app&amp;client_secret=%5BREDACTED%5D

200 OK
{"access_token":"[REDACTED]",
 "token_type":"Bearer","expires_in":3600}
            </code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p class="fragment callout danger" style="font-size: 0.55em;">Without redaction, all of this would end up in a file the workflow asks you to <b>commit</b>.</p>
    <aside class="notes">
        <p>A concrete example. On the left, the real exchange: a POST to /oauth/token, with an Authorization header in Basic, and a client_secret in the form. And in the response, an access_token.</p>
        <p>[click] On the right, what's written to the file. The Authorization header: redacted. The client_secret: redacted. The access_token: redacted.</p>
        <p>But look at what remains: the grant_type, the client_id, the token_type, the expires_in. Only the sensitive values are replaced. Everything else stays usable in your test.</p>
        <p>[click] And without this redaction, everything on the left would end up in a file the workflow explicitly asks you to commit.</p>
    </aside>
</section>
